WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.240
Right now, I mean, the smartphone sitting next to you or the computer you're listening on

00:04.240 --> 00:08.720
is processing billions of discrete electrical signals every single second.

00:09.420 --> 00:11.260
Yeah, just constantly crunching numbers.

00:11.520 --> 00:17.480
Right, and we all accept that it's undeniably doing math, but imagine if by some specific

00:17.480 --> 00:23.040
convoluted quirk of its internal network architecture, that math suddenly created a

00:23.040 --> 00:24.980
tiny enclosed loop.

00:24.980 --> 00:30.040
A loop that basically looks at the rest of the phone's hardware and decides, hey,

00:30.560 --> 00:31.600
I have a point of view.

00:31.760 --> 00:32.220
Exactly.

00:32.760 --> 00:38.420
We usually leave that kind of massive leap, the leap from cold, dead plumbing to a subjective

00:38.420 --> 00:39.320
sense of awareness.

00:39.460 --> 00:40.640
We leave that to the philosophers.

00:41.340 --> 00:47.220
But today, we're dragging it, kicking and screaming into the realm of hard, uncompromising mathematics.

00:47.720 --> 00:51.120
It really is the ultimate boundary line in science, you know?

00:51.440 --> 00:52.140
Oh, absolutely.

00:52.140 --> 00:58.720
Taking what we call the hard problem of consciousness, the mystery of how raw physical neurons firing

00:58.720 --> 01:04.540
in the dark of your skull somehow produce the vivid experience of, like, the color red or

01:04.540 --> 01:05.500
the taste of coffee.

01:05.740 --> 01:08.300
And trying to build a literal equation for it.

01:08.780 --> 01:09.040
Yeah.

01:09.660 --> 01:16.400
We are looking at a really dense, fascinating 2026 paper by the independent researcher Flyxion

01:16.400 --> 01:18.880
titled Closure Under Projection.

01:19.120 --> 01:19.380
Right.

01:19.380 --> 01:25.640
And what Flyxion is doing is critically analyzing this older, incredibly ambitious theoretical

01:25.640 --> 01:29.540
framework designed by two researchers, Marenker and Zuckerman.

01:29.940 --> 01:31.980
Often abbreviated as MZ in the literature.

01:33.000 --> 01:37.380
And the mission of our deep dive today is to really unpack this web of concepts.

01:37.840 --> 01:38.820
Because it's heavy stuff.

01:39.020 --> 01:39.440
It is.

01:39.620 --> 01:39.800
Yeah.

01:40.420 --> 01:45.380
We are going to translate extremely heavy mathematical theories, things like non-well-founded

01:45.380 --> 01:49.260
set theory and dynamical closure, into an accessible framework.

01:50.020 --> 01:55.280
So if you've ever wondered how your brain takes millions of firing neurons and compresses

01:55.280 --> 01:57.840
them into a single thought, you are going to love this.

01:57.940 --> 01:58.140
Right.

01:58.700 --> 02:03.120
Because we're living in an era where AI models are just exploding in complexity.

02:03.640 --> 02:08.380
And the question of what it takes for a network to actually possess a genuine viewpoint is,

02:08.780 --> 02:10.540
well, it's no longer science fiction.

02:10.540 --> 02:14.140
Which is why setting expectations is crucial right up front.

02:14.660 --> 02:18.600
We are not going to magically solve the hard problem of consciousness by the end of this

02:18.600 --> 02:18.820
hour.

02:19.020 --> 02:19.460
Definitely not.

02:19.600 --> 02:24.480
What Flyxion is doing, and what we're doing, is putting the proposed mathematical mechanisms

02:24.480 --> 02:25.320
under a microscope.

02:25.780 --> 02:31.460
When MZ claim they can map the utter subjectivity of the mind using absolute mathematical precision.

02:31.720 --> 02:33.900
We have to test the structural integrity of that bridge.

02:33.900 --> 02:34.340
Exactly.

02:35.080 --> 02:40.140
And to even begin building that bridge, we have to start by completely breaking the fundamental

02:40.140 --> 02:41.820
rules of traditional mathematics.

02:42.320 --> 02:42.960
We really do.

02:43.120 --> 02:44.760
Specifically the rules of set theory.

02:45.440 --> 02:49.640
Because standard math actually fails when it tries to draw a map of the brain.

02:50.000 --> 02:51.580
It absolutely hits a wall.

02:51.980 --> 02:55.900
And to understand why, we have to look at how mathematicians categorize the world.

02:56.080 --> 02:56.320
Right.

02:56.320 --> 02:57.440
They use sets.

02:57.820 --> 03:00.740
You can think of a set simply as a container.

03:01.440 --> 03:03.760
A mathematical box that holds items.

03:04.300 --> 03:05.240
Just a basic box.

03:05.460 --> 03:05.680
Yeah.

03:06.200 --> 03:10.820
And those items can be numbers, they can be objects, or they can be other smaller boxes.

03:10.820 --> 03:18.160
But in traditional classical set theory, what we call Zermelo-Frenkel set theory, there

03:18.160 --> 03:19.520
is a foundational rule.

03:20.320 --> 03:22.440
The rule of being well-founded, right?

03:22.480 --> 03:22.720
Correct.

03:23.160 --> 03:24.420
The axiom of foundation.

03:24.420 --> 03:28.300
It dictates that a box cannot contain itself.

03:29.220 --> 03:29.960
Meaning there's a bottom.

03:30.340 --> 03:30.700
Exactly.

03:30.900 --> 03:31.420
A base level.

03:31.780 --> 03:37.140
You can nest boxes infinitely downward, a box inside a box inside a box, but you can

03:37.140 --> 03:42.540
never take the largest, outermost box and shove it inside one of its own inner boxes.

03:43.060 --> 03:46.200
Because if you try, the mathematical logic creates a paradox.

03:46.680 --> 03:46.960
Right.

03:47.620 --> 03:50.100
Membership is just not allowed to circle back on itself.

03:50.580 --> 03:53.300
Which makes perfect intuitive sense to us in the physical world.

03:53.300 --> 03:57.120
I mean, I cannot put my backpack inside the side pocket of my own backpack.

03:57.700 --> 03:58.780
It's physically impossible.

03:59.060 --> 03:59.240
Right.

03:59.580 --> 04:04.320
But the brain does not operate like a collection of static, nesting physical boxes.

04:04.740 --> 04:05.140
No.

04:05.280 --> 04:06.060
It's all about loops.

04:06.860 --> 04:07.280
Exactly.

04:07.820 --> 04:12.440
The brain, and any recurrent neural network, is fundamentally defined by its loops.

04:12.440 --> 04:18.520
You have a neuron that fires, sending an electrical spike down its axon, which triggers a second

04:18.520 --> 04:18.900
neuron.

04:19.160 --> 04:20.160
Which triggers a third.

04:20.460 --> 04:23.720
And that third neuron sends a connection right back to the first one.

04:23.960 --> 04:24.860
A feedback loop.

04:25.420 --> 04:30.160
So if we try to use a traditional mathematical box to represent that first neuron, and say,

04:30.160 --> 04:34.660
okay, it contains the activity of the neurons it connects to, we suddenly have to put the

04:34.660 --> 04:36.680
first box inside the third box.

04:36.780 --> 04:38.880
Which is already inside the first box.

04:39.200 --> 04:39.460
Right.

04:39.940 --> 04:42.740
And classical math just throws its hands up and crashes.

04:43.200 --> 04:44.180
That is wild.

04:44.840 --> 04:49.260
And that's exactly why Marenker and Zuckerman had to abandon classical set theory, right?

04:49.920 --> 04:50.200
Yeah.

04:50.440 --> 04:55.400
They adopted a radical alternative proposed in 1988 by the mathematician Peter Axel.

04:55.560 --> 04:55.860
Okay.

04:55.860 --> 04:59.900
Axel introduced what he called the anti-foundation axiom.

05:00.540 --> 05:04.960
He effectively rewrote the rules to say, you know, what if we remove that restriction

05:04.960 --> 05:05.680
entirely?

05:06.120 --> 05:08.140
What if we allow circular membership?

05:08.920 --> 05:10.600
The anti-foundation axiom.

05:10.900 --> 05:14.880
So under Axel's rules, a set can actually be a member of itself?

05:15.440 --> 05:15.900
It can.

05:16.600 --> 05:20.880
The most elegant, stripped-down example of this is a structure called a quine atom.

05:21.200 --> 05:22.380
A quine atom.

05:22.380 --> 05:22.860
Yeah.

05:23.000 --> 05:27.780
It's usually written out as an equation, Q equals the set containing Q.

05:28.320 --> 05:28.620
Wow.

05:28.840 --> 05:32.460
The entire definition of the set is just itself, looping infinitely.

05:32.940 --> 05:37.920
If I'm trying to visualize this, classical math is like walking down a hallway with a

05:37.920 --> 05:38.780
clear beginning and end.

05:39.460 --> 05:43.700
But Axel's math is like stepping between two opposing mirrors in a funhouse.

05:43.920 --> 05:45.920
Oh, that's a great way to look at it.

05:46.060 --> 05:49.880
You look to your left and the reflection contains the reflection, which contains the reflection

05:49.880 --> 05:51.020
recursively forever.

05:51.020 --> 05:52.860
There's no base level.

05:53.040 --> 05:54.860
It's a pure infinite loop.

05:55.360 --> 05:58.000
The mirror analogy captures the geometry perfectly.

05:58.840 --> 06:03.760
And what Marenker and Zuckerman do is take this concept of the infinite recursive mirror

06:03.760 --> 06:07.500
hall and map it onto the physical architecture of a neural network.

06:07.740 --> 06:07.960
Okay.

06:08.000 --> 06:08.680
How do they do that?

06:09.200 --> 06:14.240
They assign every single node in the network, every neuron, a mathematical set, which they

06:14.240 --> 06:15.120
call its decoration.

06:15.520 --> 06:20.160
Like assigning a highly complex mathematical name tag to every single physical neuron in the

06:20.160 --> 06:20.420
brain?

06:20.420 --> 06:23.700
A name tag that is constructed by looking at its neighbors.

06:24.320 --> 06:30.580
The MZ equation states that the decoration of a given node, let's call it node A, is formed

06:30.580 --> 06:35.980
by collecting the decorations of all the nodes that node A sends signals to.

06:35.980 --> 06:43.300
Okay, so if node A points an arrow to node B, then node A's mathematical box must contain

06:43.300 --> 06:44.540
node B's box.

06:44.680 --> 06:45.040
Precisely.

06:45.540 --> 06:52.080
But because we have loops, if A points to B and B points to A, then A's box contains B and

06:52.080 --> 06:56.480
B's box contains A. We instantly create that infinitely reflecting mirror.

06:56.920 --> 06:58.960
We create a non-well-founded set.

06:58.960 --> 06:59.760
Wow.

07:00.340 --> 07:04.280
But wait, this brilliant workaround introduces a pretty massive headache, right?

07:04.360 --> 07:06.040
Both philosophically and mathematically.

07:06.560 --> 07:07.260
It really does.

07:07.380 --> 07:12.960
Because under Axel's anti-foundation logic, the identity of a set is determined purely by

07:12.960 --> 07:14.640
its structure, by its shape.

07:15.140 --> 07:15.500
Okay.

07:15.980 --> 07:20.760
So if you have two completely distinct physical nodes located on opposite sides of a network,

07:20.760 --> 07:27.120
but they happen to be part of identical local connection structures, say, two isolated pairs

07:27.120 --> 07:29.560
of neurons that just point at each other and nothing else.

07:30.180 --> 07:33.420
Axel's math looks at them and declares they are the exact same set.

07:33.780 --> 07:34.180
Exactly.

07:34.960 --> 07:36.940
Wait, let me make sure I follow the implication here.

07:37.020 --> 07:43.400
You're saying that if MZ just used the network's wiring to define the set, then two completely

07:43.400 --> 07:45.680
different physical things are merged into one identity.

07:46.160 --> 07:46.520
Yes.

07:46.520 --> 07:51.340
It would be like saying two identical suburban houses, one in California and one in New York,

07:51.540 --> 07:56.380
are mathematically the exact same house, just because their architectural blueprints happen

07:56.380 --> 07:57.660
to loop in the same way.

07:58.060 --> 08:01.560
That is the consequence of structural bisimilarity.

08:01.860 --> 08:05.740
From the perspective of pure graph topology, the California house and the New York house

08:05.740 --> 08:06.620
are indistinguishable.

08:07.240 --> 08:09.300
You would only see one mathematical object.

08:09.840 --> 08:14.980
But for a model trying to represent the dynamic physical reality of a brain, where physical

08:14.980 --> 08:19.960
location and specific local activity matter immensely, that structural blindness feels

08:19.960 --> 08:20.820
like a fatal flaw.

08:21.600 --> 08:22.100
It would be.

08:22.580 --> 08:27.200
Because if the network is busy processing the visual image of a lion, and a completely different

08:27.200 --> 08:29.800
part of the network is processing the sound of a rustling bush...

08:30.440 --> 08:32.360
But their local wiring happens to match.

08:33.000 --> 08:34.580
The math would just smush them together.

08:35.060 --> 08:36.600
We would lose the actual data.

08:37.480 --> 08:38.740
Okay, so how do they fix that?

08:39.260 --> 08:42.000
That's why the MZ equation has a crucial second half.

08:42.000 --> 08:45.740
The decoration of node A isn't just the sets of its children.

08:46.060 --> 08:50.160
It also unites that structural shape with a second component, labeled H of A.

08:50.620 --> 08:54.240
H of A. And the H stands for a histogram?

08:54.640 --> 08:54.920
Yes.

08:55.600 --> 08:58.200
A histogram of the active incoming synaptic weights.

08:59.180 --> 09:00.560
A histogram of what, exactly?

09:01.100 --> 09:02.080
Like, what is it measuring?

09:02.660 --> 09:08.320
It's measuring the actual momentary electrical activity arriving at that specific node.

09:08.420 --> 09:09.240
Oh, okay.

09:09.240 --> 09:14.820
It might say, I am receiving 15 units of signal from one connection and three units from another.

09:15.280 --> 09:18.760
And the critical thing about this H of A label is that it is well-founded.

09:19.020 --> 09:19.920
It has a bottom.

09:20.120 --> 09:20.280
Right.

09:20.400 --> 09:23.600
It is standard, non-looping, classical data.

09:23.920 --> 09:24.800
Ah, I see.

09:25.500 --> 09:28.840
The H of A label is the street address on those identical houses.

09:29.140 --> 09:30.000
That's it, exactly.

09:30.660 --> 09:31.940
It's the physical tiebreaker.

09:31.940 --> 09:37.740
By attaching the local electrical activity to the infinite loop, MZ can say, yes, these

09:37.740 --> 09:42.500
two subnetworks have the exact same infinite mirror of reflection, but the one in the visual

09:42.500 --> 09:48.240
cortex has 50 millivolts running through it, and the one in the auditory cortex has 10 millivolts.

09:48.240 --> 09:52.560
The activity data anchors the abstract loop to physical reality.

09:52.900 --> 09:55.680
It grounds the infinite math in finite physics.

09:56.080 --> 09:56.640
That is brilliant.

09:57.140 --> 10:01.060
And when you combine the looping structure with those local labels, you get a highly specific

10:01.060 --> 10:02.140
mathematical object.

10:02.620 --> 10:03.820
MZ have terms for these.

10:04.100 --> 10:04.360
Okay.

10:04.620 --> 10:05.140
Lay them on me.

10:05.140 --> 10:11.060
When you take an entire directed graph, decorate all its nodes this way, and pick one specific

10:11.060 --> 10:15.540
node as your starting vantage point, they call that overall structure a meme.

10:15.940 --> 10:16.540
A meme.

10:17.080 --> 10:19.480
Obviously not in the sense of a viral internet picture.

10:19.760 --> 10:24.020
No, in the original Richard Dawkins sense of a structural unit of information.

10:24.260 --> 10:24.540
Right.

10:24.860 --> 10:29.820
And the specific, highly complex mathematical set that decorates your chosen starting node,

10:29.980 --> 10:33.000
the vantage point of the whole structure, they call it the thema.

10:33.320 --> 10:34.240
The thema.

10:34.240 --> 10:38.100
Yes, and this is where Marenker and Zuckerman make their boldest claim.

10:38.360 --> 10:43.940
They propose that this mathematical object, the thema, represents a primary experience.

10:44.220 --> 10:44.760
Wait, what?

10:45.520 --> 10:50.780
They are arguing that by mathematically encapsulating a specific vantage point within a recurrent network,

10:50.980 --> 10:54.560
they have defined the structure of a subjective experience.

10:55.020 --> 10:57.040
That is a staggering leap.

10:57.480 --> 11:01.560
They are literally drawing an equation on a chalkboard and labeling it an experience.

11:02.120 --> 11:02.620
They are?

11:02.620 --> 11:08.780
Before we even touch the philosophical trapdoors hidden in that claim, I want to look at the mechanics of it.

11:09.360 --> 11:14.460
Because if this thema structure is an infinitely looping hall of mirrors and the local activity data,

11:14.680 --> 11:19.060
the street addresses are just thrown into that infinite recursive blender.

11:19.060 --> 11:21.380
How do we ever get the useful data back out?

11:21.780 --> 11:22.120
Yes.

11:22.440 --> 11:28.760
Because if I hand you a locked safe and say, hey, the secrets of consciousness are in here, but the safe can never be opened.

11:29.160 --> 11:30.400
You haven't done science.

11:30.540 --> 11:30.800
Right.

11:30.960 --> 11:32.200
I've just done performance art.

11:32.480 --> 11:33.200
It's a great point.

11:33.200 --> 11:39.500
And you are hitting on the central mechanical problem of their theory, which Flyxion tackles in Proposition 2.1.

11:39.960 --> 11:41.600
The problem of recoverability.

11:42.000 --> 11:43.000
Unscrambling the egg.

11:43.560 --> 11:44.200
Exactly.

11:44.740 --> 11:52.460
To crack the safe, the original MZ paper utilizes a specialized mathematical selector operation denoted as W of X.

11:52.560 --> 11:53.180
W of X.

11:53.740 --> 11:55.880
The function of W is elegantly simple.

11:56.500 --> 12:01.720
It's a filter that looks at a set and strictly preserves only the elements that are well-founded.

12:01.900 --> 12:03.040
So anything that loops.

12:03.440 --> 12:07.320
Anything that relies on the anti-foundation axiom is summarily deleted.

12:08.020 --> 12:08.680
Oh, that is clever.

12:08.680 --> 12:19.160
So we have this massive decoration, which is a combination of the non-well-founded network architecture, the infinite mirrors, and the well-founded histograms, the actual voltage data.

12:19.680 --> 12:19.960
Right.

12:20.040 --> 12:23.960
And the mechanism operates like a sieve designed to catch only finite objects.

12:24.460 --> 12:29.720
When you push the Thaimas decoration through the W filter, the infinite graph structure falls right through.

12:29.980 --> 12:30.980
The loops are just deleted.

12:31.520 --> 12:35.280
And what remains stuck in the sieve is exclusively the well-founded label.

12:35.280 --> 12:41.540
Mathematically, the operation W applied to the decoration of node A simply equals H of A.

12:42.140 --> 12:49.700
You strip away the entire universe of network architecture, and you're left holding the exact histogram of local activity data that you started with.

12:49.900 --> 12:50.720
It works perfectly.

12:51.040 --> 12:53.020
It feels like a mathematical magic trick.

12:53.480 --> 12:57.940
You bury the data in infinity, and then use a custom filter to yank it right back out.

12:57.940 --> 13:06.540
But knowing how mathematical models map to biological reality, I have to assume this magic trick has some pretty severe conditions attached to it.

13:06.900 --> 13:12.120
Oh, Flyxion is unrelenting in pointing out exactly how fragile this recovery trick actually is.

13:12.720 --> 13:13.980
It's not a universal law.

13:14.280 --> 13:16.340
It is a highly contingent feature.

13:16.980 --> 13:17.560
What's the catch?

13:17.560 --> 13:25.680
For the W filter to successfully isolate the histogram, there must be absolute, unambiguous separation between the data and the structure.

13:26.240 --> 13:32.040
This means every single neighboring decoration that makes up the network architecture must be non-well-founded.

13:32.520 --> 13:33.760
They must all loop.

13:34.400 --> 13:35.260
Every single one.

13:36.000 --> 13:42.960
Because if they don't loop, let's say our network has a dead end, a neuron that receives a signal but doesn't pass it on to anything else.

13:43.400 --> 13:45.100
Then that node isn't part of a loop.

13:45.100 --> 13:48.580
So its mathematical decoration wouldn't be infinite, right?

13:49.180 --> 13:49.980
It would have a bottom.

13:50.460 --> 13:51.980
It would be well-founded.

13:52.580 --> 13:58.780
And if the structural decoration is well-founded, the W sieve cannot tell the difference between the structure and the histogram data.

13:59.220 --> 13:59.640
Oh, no.

14:00.340 --> 14:03.800
The dead end node gets caught in the filter alongside the local voltages.

14:04.460 --> 14:09.140
The clean separation completely collapses, and you can no longer extract the pure activity state.

14:09.680 --> 14:13.740
That seems like an insurmountable problem for applying this to an actual human brain.

14:13.740 --> 14:16.600
I mean, the brain is not a perfectly sealed loop.

14:17.340 --> 14:17.820
Far from it.

14:18.100 --> 14:20.600
It has sensory inputs coming in from the optic nerve.

14:20.720 --> 14:23.160
It has motor outputs terminating in the muscles.

14:23.300 --> 14:25.180
It's full of dead ends and linear pathways.

14:25.520 --> 14:33.040
Which is exactly why, to make the math work, MZ were forced to hypothesize incredibly strict, almost artificial constraints.

14:33.240 --> 14:33.580
Like what?

14:33.580 --> 14:39.160
They only look at finite multigraphs, where every single node has at least one outgoing arrow.

14:39.680 --> 14:47.120
They have to mathematically mandate that every single point in the brain participates in a perpetual loop, just to ensure the recovery filter functions.

14:47.360 --> 14:52.120
Which means we're already drifting away from biological reality and into idealized geometry.

14:52.940 --> 14:53.620
We really are.

14:54.340 --> 14:56.160
And the limitations compound from there.

14:56.720 --> 15:01.760
Flyxion clarifies that even when the filter works perfectly, we need to be honest about what is actually being recovered.

15:01.760 --> 15:03.560
The labels, right?

15:04.200 --> 15:08.260
Yes, but only the labels on a static, fixed graph.

15:08.660 --> 15:11.300
It completely destroys the topology of the graph itself.

15:11.680 --> 15:14.880
If you only have the recovered labels, you cannot rebuild the network.

15:15.220 --> 15:18.020
You just have random street addresses with no map.

15:18.600 --> 15:18.940
Right.

15:19.300 --> 15:19.540
Yeah.

15:20.120 --> 15:26.660
Furthermore, recovering a histogram of incoming weights does not recover the identities of the nodes that sent those weights.

15:27.060 --> 15:29.940
The distinct origins of the signals are permanently lost.

15:30.080 --> 15:31.600
Wait, let me chew on that for a second.

15:32.460 --> 15:35.740
Because I want to push back on this whole idea of recovery.

15:36.360 --> 15:39.080
I think there's a bit of a sleight of hand happening here.

15:39.960 --> 15:41.040
Okay, let's hear it.

15:41.640 --> 15:44.000
Let's say we grant them their perfectly looping multigraph.

15:45.100 --> 15:54.740
Let's say a mathematician standing outside the system can successfully use this W sieve to extract the H of a label and write it on a whiteboard.

15:55.060 --> 15:55.340
Sure.

15:55.340 --> 16:01.660
Does the neural network itself actually possess the ability to read that label?

16:02.660 --> 16:07.080
This is perhaps the most devastating part of Flyxion's critique regarding the static model.

16:07.440 --> 16:10.420
It centers on conflating the map with the territory.

16:10.900 --> 16:11.220
Right.

16:11.220 --> 16:14.460
Flyxion demands that we separate four distinct concepts.

16:15.040 --> 16:19.180
Encoding, access, use, and sufficiency.

16:19.180 --> 16:20.800
Let's walk through those.

16:21.640 --> 16:29.200
Because just proving that data is encoded inside a mathematical structure doesn't automatically grant the physical system access to it.

16:29.200 --> 16:32.540
The analogy of a computer file works really well here.

16:33.100 --> 16:37.400
You can take a complex video file and encode it onto a USB flash drive.

16:37.520 --> 16:38.440
The encoding is perfect.

16:38.440 --> 16:41.880
The data is physically present on the drive.

16:41.880 --> 16:51.200
But if you plug that USB drive into a computer that lacks an operating system or lacks the specific codec required to unpack the video format...

16:51.200 --> 16:52.960
The computer has no access to the data.

16:53.380 --> 16:55.440
It's encoded, but inaccessible.

16:56.320 --> 16:56.880
Exactly.

16:57.720 --> 16:59.460
I'll take your analogy a step further.

16:59.460 --> 17:07.840
Even if the computer has the right operating system and the right video player, so it has access, that still doesn't prove it's going to use it correctly.

17:08.600 --> 17:08.960
What do you mean?

17:09.620 --> 17:18.840
Well, I could write a script that opens the video file, but instead of playing the movie on the screen, it just counts the number of times the letter A appears in the file's binary code.

17:19.360 --> 17:19.720
Ugh.

17:19.980 --> 17:25.560
The system is accessing the data, but its use has absolutely nothing to do with the experience of watching the video.

17:26.060 --> 17:28.920
That distinction is exactly what Flyxion is highlighting.

17:28.920 --> 17:35.000
The Emsi Recovery Theorem, mathematically elegant as it is, only accomplishes step one.

17:35.680 --> 17:36.900
It just proves the data is there.

17:37.480 --> 17:44.840
It proves that the local activity data isn't mathematically annihilated when it gets folded into the infinite, non-well-founded structure.

17:45.160 --> 17:55.680
It is preserved as an encoding, but that provides zero evidence that the network dynamically uses that encoded data to generate what they are calling a primary experience.

17:55.680 --> 18:00.960
Proving the data exists is miles away from proving the data acts as the engine of consciousness.

18:01.460 --> 18:01.880
Exactly.

18:02.740 --> 18:10.000
And all of this debate, the encoding, the filters, the USB drives, it's all happening while the system is basically paused.

18:10.000 --> 18:17.420
We've just been looking at a single frozen snapshot in time, a mathematician analyzing a static equation.

18:17.820 --> 18:18.760
But minds are not static.

18:19.000 --> 18:19.140
Right.

18:19.360 --> 18:20.780
AI models are not static.

18:20.960 --> 18:22.260
They process over time.

18:22.360 --> 18:24.760
They are dynamic flows of energy.

18:25.020 --> 18:29.460
Which forces us to take this mathematical model off pause and hit play.

18:29.460 --> 18:33.920
And that transition brings us to the transported dynamics.

18:34.660 --> 18:37.260
Which is really the undeniable heart of Flyxion's paper.

18:38.000 --> 18:41.700
How does this static set theory cope with the passage of time?

18:42.300 --> 18:45.840
If we're going to set the network in motion, what rules are we even using?

18:45.920 --> 18:48.440
Because neurons don't just randomly change their voltages.

18:48.800 --> 18:49.140
Right.

18:49.140 --> 18:59.860
To establish a baseline for movement, MZ rely on one of the foundational pillars of computational neuroscience, the McCulloch-Pitts model of a binary threshold neuron.

19:00.020 --> 19:01.260
The McCulloch-Pitts model.

19:01.660 --> 19:04.060
It's an older model, but mathematically rigorous.

19:04.660 --> 19:06.260
It operates on a very simple premise.

19:06.580 --> 19:11.660
A neuron receives incoming signals, each with a specific weight, and it sums them up.

19:11.840 --> 19:12.980
It aggregates the pressure.

19:13.200 --> 19:13.600
It does.

19:13.600 --> 19:16.500
And the neuron has a built-in threshold.

19:16.960 --> 19:20.680
The math uses a function called the heavy side step function to represent this.

19:20.880 --> 19:21.920
The heavy side step function?

19:22.420 --> 19:24.860
You can think of it like a physical dam on a river.

19:25.580 --> 19:29.380
As the incoming signals aggregate, the water level behind the dam rises.

19:29.880 --> 19:30.140
Okay.

19:30.580 --> 19:34.800
As long as the water is below the top of the dam, the output is exactly zero.

19:35.180 --> 19:36.700
The neuron is totally silent.

19:36.940 --> 19:40.760
But the millisecond that the aggregate activity reaches the threshold.

19:41.120 --> 19:42.280
The water spills over the dam.

19:42.280 --> 19:45.380
The function instantly outputs a one, and the neuron fires.

19:45.760 --> 19:46.460
A binary state.

19:46.880 --> 19:47.740
Off or on.

19:48.620 --> 19:54.820
And in the specific version of the model MZ are using here, the physical pipes, the synaptic

19:54.820 --> 19:57.440
weights between the neurons, those are locked in place, right?

19:57.920 --> 19:58.160
Yes.

19:58.280 --> 19:59.220
The architecture is fixed.

19:59.940 --> 20:05.000
So the only thing changing over time is the water level behind the dams, the actual firing

20:05.000 --> 20:05.580
activity.

20:05.940 --> 20:07.580
The physical voltages are updating.

20:07.580 --> 20:13.400
And the challenge for MZ is that they have to prove their abstract infinite set theory

20:13.400 --> 20:18.420
math updates in perfect synchronization with those physical McCulloch-Pitt stamps.

20:19.120 --> 20:23.860
They need to transport the physical dynamics into the abstract space.

20:24.360 --> 20:24.840
Precisely.

20:25.260 --> 20:31.620
They formulate this with the equation S equals A sub gamma times T times A sub gamma inverse.

20:31.620 --> 20:37.300
Okay, let's demystify that formula because throwing inverse matrices around can lose people

20:37.300 --> 20:37.960
really quickly.

20:38.300 --> 20:38.640
Fair enough.

20:38.860 --> 20:41.900
Let's look at the mechanics of what those symbols are actually doing.

20:42.140 --> 20:45.640
The best way to understand it is as a process of continuous translation.

20:46.300 --> 20:49.580
The symbol A sub gamma represents the translation mechanism.

20:50.240 --> 20:55.120
It's the dictionary that takes the physical reality, the water levels behind the dams, and

20:55.120 --> 20:59.080
translates them into the abstract, non-well-founded decorations, the famous.

20:59.080 --> 21:08.240
So, step one, A sub gamma inverse, is taking the physical state of the brain at, say, 12.00pm

21:08.240 --> 21:12.620
and translating it into a massive set theory equation on the chalkboard.

21:12.960 --> 21:13.240
Yes.

21:13.940 --> 21:14.240
Okay.

21:14.640 --> 21:17.200
And once it's on the chalkboard, you apply T.

21:17.720 --> 21:18.620
And T is what?

21:18.980 --> 21:23.300
T is the abstract mathematical rule for how the sets should evolve over the next millisecond.

21:23.720 --> 21:26.800
It's the update function operating purely in the realm of math.

21:26.800 --> 21:34.960
Okay, and once the math has updated the state to 12.00.01pm on the chalkboard, we have to

21:34.960 --> 21:37.200
see if it matches reality.

21:37.740 --> 21:37.940
Right.

21:38.060 --> 21:42.360
So, we use A sub gamma to translate the new mathematical state back down into physical

21:42.360 --> 21:42.820
voltages.

21:43.640 --> 21:46.580
And what Mz proved is that the loop holds.

21:47.100 --> 21:47.600
It holds.

21:47.600 --> 21:52.800
If you let the physical brain update naturally, or if you translate it into math, update the

21:52.800 --> 21:56.680
math and translate it back, you arrive at the exact same physical result.

21:56.920 --> 22:00.480
The abstract model perfectly shadows the physical reality.

22:00.700 --> 22:01.120
It does.

22:01.860 --> 22:04.900
But proving the math works on the whole system wasn't the goal.

22:05.580 --> 22:09.540
The goal was to prove the math works for a subjective point of view.

22:09.540 --> 22:14.420
And this is where Phlaxion introduces a massive pivot in the analysis.

22:15.380 --> 22:20.700
This is the concept that gives the entire paper its title, Dynamical Closure of a Projection.

22:21.100 --> 22:25.200
Mathematically, this is expressed as pi S equals F pi.

22:26.080 --> 22:30.480
And to understand why this is the crucible for any theory of consciousness, we really need

22:30.480 --> 22:32.780
to dissect what a projection actually is.

22:32.780 --> 22:34.840
Let's break down the symbols again.

22:35.440 --> 22:38.100
The Greek letter pi, that stands for the projection.

22:38.980 --> 22:42.280
A projection is inherently an act of extreme compression.

22:42.600 --> 22:42.760
Okay.

22:43.180 --> 22:48.340
You are taking a massive, multidimensional, highly complex system, like the billions of

22:48.340 --> 22:53.260
firing neurons in an entire brain, and you are compressing it down to a much smaller,

22:53.460 --> 22:54.620
localized viewpoint.

22:55.020 --> 22:57.640
You are projecting the whole onto a part.

22:58.140 --> 23:02.360
So, by definition, a projection involves a massive loss of information.

23:02.360 --> 23:02.940
Huge loss.

23:03.520 --> 23:07.560
It's the mathematical equivalent of taking a complex, three-dimensional object, like

23:07.560 --> 23:12.600
a carved statue, and shining a flashlight on it to cast a two-dimensional shadow on the

23:12.600 --> 23:12.840
wall.

23:12.880 --> 23:13.240
Exactly.

23:13.920 --> 23:15.160
The shadow is a projection.

23:15.880 --> 23:20.220
It captures the outline, but it loses the depth, the texture, the color.

23:20.700 --> 23:23.140
And the shadow is exactly what Emsi's thema is.

23:23.500 --> 23:28.120
It is the compressed, mathematically localized viewpoint of the entire network.

23:28.540 --> 23:30.720
Now, let's look at the two sides of Phlaxion's equation.

23:30.720 --> 23:32.500
Pi S equals F pi.

23:33.160 --> 23:38.680
On the left side, we have pi S. S, remember, is the update of the full, massive system.

23:39.200 --> 23:44.720
So pi S means, let the entire physical brain update for one millisecond, with all of its

23:44.720 --> 23:45.500
infinite complexity.

23:45.960 --> 23:47.420
And then, shine the flashlight.

23:48.180 --> 23:49.760
Cast the shadow of the new state.

23:50.160 --> 23:50.540
Exactly.

23:51.100 --> 23:53.560
Now, on the right side, we have F pi.

23:53.560 --> 23:59.580
F represents a localized, isolated update rule that only applies to the compressed shadow.

24:00.180 --> 24:01.080
It doesn't know about the statue.

24:01.540 --> 24:01.780
No.

24:01.940 --> 24:03.480
It only knows about the 2D shadow.

24:03.900 --> 24:06.900
So F pi means, shine the flashlight right now to get the shadow.

24:07.600 --> 24:12.040
And then, try to calculate what the shadow will do in the next millisecond, using only

24:12.040 --> 24:16.440
the information contained within the shadow itself, completely ignoring the 3D statue.

24:16.440 --> 24:17.600
You've got it.

24:18.020 --> 24:24.620
And if pi S equals f pi, if both processes yield the exact same future shadow, then the

24:24.620 --> 24:26.320
system possesses dynamical closure.

24:26.840 --> 24:28.780
This is the critical aha moment.

24:29.380 --> 24:34.960
If closure holds, it means the 2D shadow contains every single piece of information necessary

24:34.960 --> 24:36.540
to predict its own future.

24:37.000 --> 24:39.200
It doesn't need to consult the full 3D reality.

24:39.580 --> 24:44.220
The subjective, compressed viewpoint is an autonomous, self-contained system.

24:44.220 --> 24:44.700
Yes.

24:45.060 --> 24:50.060
And conversely, if the equation fails, if closure does not hold, it means the shadow

24:50.060 --> 24:52.780
is mathematically blind to the forces controlling it.

24:53.540 --> 24:55.660
Let's ground this in a real-world analogy.

24:56.420 --> 25:00.120
Imagine you're watching a heavily compressed weather forecast video on your phone.

25:00.300 --> 25:00.600
Okay.

25:01.020 --> 25:02.040
The video is a projection.

25:02.540 --> 25:07.780
It takes billions of data points, satellite telemetry, ocean temperatures, atmospheric pressure,

25:08.180 --> 25:13.780
and compresses it all down into a 2-minute clip of a person pointing at a map with some

25:13.780 --> 25:15.500
sun and rain icons.

25:16.060 --> 25:17.280
So the video is the thema.

25:17.740 --> 25:18.200
Right.

25:19.240 --> 25:24.460
If the weather video possesses dynamical closure, it means that a meteorologist could look at

25:24.460 --> 25:30.280
today's 2-minute video, completely ignore the actual ocean and satellites, and perfectly

25:30.280 --> 25:35.000
calculate what tomorrow's video will look like using only the pixels in today's video.

25:35.500 --> 25:39.460
Which sounds impossible because we know how much information was lost in the compression.

25:39.460 --> 25:40.020
Right.

25:41.020 --> 25:45.820
If closure fails, you could have a situation where you watch two weather videos from two

25:45.820 --> 25:46.620
parallel Earths.

25:47.240 --> 25:49.620
Both videos show identical sunny skies over Miami.

25:50.160 --> 25:53.200
From the viewpoint of the video, the states are mathematically identical.

25:54.060 --> 25:59.140
But tomorrow, Earth A is still sunny, while Earth B gets hit by a Category 5 hurricane.

25:59.680 --> 26:00.000
Why?

26:00.680 --> 26:05.000
Because on Earth B, there was a massive drop in barometric pressure happening just off the

26:05.000 --> 26:05.300
coast.

26:05.300 --> 26:08.520
But the barometric pressure data was cut out during the compression.

26:09.200 --> 26:10.240
It was a hidden variable.

26:10.740 --> 26:11.260
Exactly.

26:12.020 --> 26:15.560
That analogy perfectly isolates the danger of incomplete projections.

26:16.220 --> 26:21.520
When a projection lacks dynamical closure, identical subjective states will inexplicably diverge

26:21.520 --> 26:25.840
into completely different futures, driven by hidden machinery outside the viewpoint.

26:26.280 --> 26:26.600
Wow.

26:26.600 --> 26:32.660
And Flyxion is incredibly meticulous about cataloging exactly what raw data gets left on the cutting

26:32.660 --> 26:35.820
room floor when MZ compressed the brain down to the thema.

26:36.160 --> 26:37.920
Let's list out those hidden variables.

26:38.620 --> 26:40.540
What specific information is the shadow losing?

26:41.080 --> 26:44.260
There are three primary categories of lost information.

26:44.940 --> 26:47.940
The first we actually touched on earlier, by similar notes.

26:48.240 --> 26:49.500
The structural shape issue.

26:49.500 --> 26:54.900
Because the math relies on structural shape, nodes that are physically distinct but structurally

26:54.900 --> 26:58.980
identical are merged into a single mathematical element within the thema.

26:59.500 --> 27:01.440
The viewpoint literally cannot tell them apart.

27:01.880 --> 27:03.580
It treats two separate engines as one.

27:04.020 --> 27:08.900
It's like the weather video replacing two distinct storm fronts with a single generic rain icon.

27:09.520 --> 27:11.760
You lose the nuance of where the rain actually is.

27:11.940 --> 27:14.780
The second loss occurs at the local activity level.

27:14.780 --> 27:20.520
The exact identity of the firing pre-synaptic neurons is lost to the H of a histogram.

27:21.420 --> 27:22.400
Because it just adds them up.

27:22.640 --> 27:22.840
Right.

27:23.000 --> 27:25.260
The viewpoint only receives the aggregate volume.

27:25.840 --> 27:29.660
It sees that five units of signal arrived, but it's completely blind to whether those signals

27:29.660 --> 27:33.060
came from neurons A, B, and C or neurons X, Y, and Z.

27:33.440 --> 27:38.800
So the viewpoint can hear the roar of the crowd, but it's mathematically impossible for it to

27:38.800 --> 27:40.360
pick out a specific voice.

27:40.920 --> 27:43.500
It has lost the origin data.

27:43.500 --> 27:44.180
It has.

27:44.780 --> 27:46.500
And the third loss is the most severe.

27:47.120 --> 27:51.120
Any part of the massive neural network that cannot be reached by tracing directed arrows

27:51.120 --> 27:53.540
backward from the specific viewpoint node...

27:53.540 --> 27:54.560
Wait, what happens to it?

27:54.940 --> 27:57.860
Mathematically, it ceases to exist within the thema's decoration.

27:58.280 --> 28:02.380
If there's no wire pointing directly from that part of the brain to our specific localized

28:02.380 --> 28:04.160
node, it's totally invisible.

28:04.540 --> 28:05.860
It is a complete blind spot.

28:06.520 --> 28:11.260
Now, imagine a massive storm of electrical activity brewing in one of those blind spots.

28:11.260 --> 28:14.480
From the perspective of the thema, everything is calm.

28:14.600 --> 28:16.300
The shadow is stable.

28:16.520 --> 28:21.700
But a millisecond later, that unseen storm propagates through the network, hits the viewpoint node,

28:21.700 --> 28:23.240
and completely alters its state.

28:23.240 --> 28:26.920
The hurricane hits Miami, and the weather video had no idea it was coming.

28:27.260 --> 28:31.520
The viewpoint couldn't predict its own future because it was acting on incomplete information.

28:31.520 --> 28:41.980
This is why Flyxion elevates dynamical closure from a neat mathematical trick to the ultimate, uncompromising acid test for any scientific model of consciousness.

28:42.400 --> 28:49.700
If a localized point of view is dynamically closed, it means the subjective experience is a complete causal story.

28:49.700 --> 28:52.580
It can be studied and understood on its own terms.

28:52.980 --> 28:53.340
Exactly.

28:53.980 --> 28:59.760
But if it is not closed, if it constantly relies on these hidden variables, then a point of view

28:59.760 --> 29:01.140
isn't a robust phenomenon.

29:01.580 --> 29:06.220
It's just a leaky, incomplete illusion that is entirely at the mercy of physical machinery

29:06.220 --> 29:08.120
the subject isn't even aware of.

29:08.540 --> 29:12.260
And this is where the friction with Marunker and Zuckerman becomes acute.

29:12.940 --> 29:14.740
Because they don't see it that way.

29:15.460 --> 29:15.820
No.

29:16.720 --> 29:19.640
MZ do not treat their thema as a leaky illusion.

29:19.940 --> 29:22.100
They treat it as a foundational reality.

29:22.600 --> 29:28.540
They want to take this mathematical shadow and apply what they literally call consciousness operators to it

29:28.540 --> 29:30.180
to prove it generates awareness.

29:30.740 --> 29:33.460
Which takes us right into the heart of the philosophical battlefield.

29:34.300 --> 29:37.280
We are looking at how mathematicians try to define awareness.

29:37.520 --> 29:37.760
Right.

29:38.840 --> 29:42.260
MZ propose a set of four rigid axioms.

29:42.260 --> 29:47.780
They argue that if any mathematical operator satisfies these four rules, it is by definition

29:47.780 --> 29:51.200
generating awareness out of a primary experience.

29:51.940 --> 29:55.440
Let's examine the architecture of this claim because it is bold.

29:56.280 --> 29:59.420
They denote this proposed consciousness operator with the letter K.

29:59.920 --> 30:02.740
And the K operator must adhere to four laws.

30:03.360 --> 30:03.620
Okay.

30:03.680 --> 30:04.600
Let's unpack these laws.

30:05.060 --> 30:06.940
The first law is generation.

30:06.940 --> 30:12.160
It dictates that the awareness must be a mathematical subset of the experience.

30:12.820 --> 30:17.200
You cannot be aware of something that is not already present within the boundary of the experience.

30:18.100 --> 30:19.660
That feels relatively logical.

30:20.320 --> 30:23.340
I mean, you can't remember a detail from a movie you never watched.

30:23.960 --> 30:27.180
The awareness has to be built from the raw material of the experience.

30:27.480 --> 30:27.680
Right.

30:28.540 --> 30:30.740
The second law is irreversibility.

30:30.740 --> 30:35.200
It states that the raw experience itself cannot be a member of the awareness.

30:36.320 --> 30:37.480
Let me puzzle that out.

30:38.440 --> 30:42.140
I think this means that awareness is inherently a step removed from raw data.

30:42.740 --> 30:43.060
How so?

30:43.400 --> 30:46.360
Like if the experience is the raw data of touching a hot stove.

30:47.240 --> 30:50.420
The awareness is the localized thought, my hand is burning.

30:51.380 --> 30:54.880
The thought is derived from the data, but the thought is not the raw data itself.

30:55.260 --> 30:55.900
They're distinct.

30:56.400 --> 30:58.020
That is a very solid interpretation.

30:58.020 --> 31:03.060
Now, the third law, removal, enforces that separation from the other direction.

31:03.740 --> 31:07.240
It dictates that the awareness cannot be a member of the experience.

31:07.680 --> 31:10.520
The two mathematical entities must remain cleanly separated.

31:10.980 --> 31:12.440
One cannot subsume the other.

31:12.820 --> 31:14.660
So they can't bleed into each other mathematically.

31:14.940 --> 31:15.240
Exactly.

31:15.740 --> 31:21.820
But the fourth law, selection, is where the mathematics get highly specific and where the trap is set.

31:22.020 --> 31:22.580
What's the rule?

31:22.580 --> 31:34.320
The law states, if one experience is a subset of a larger experience, the awareness of the first must be perfectly equal to the first experience intersecting with the awareness of the larger.

31:34.660 --> 31:35.100
Wow.

31:35.540 --> 31:37.880
That is a dense tangle of terminology.

31:38.320 --> 31:39.360
Let's untangle it.

31:39.360 --> 31:55.880
If my first experience is looking at a tree and my larger experience is looking at a forest, this law dictates that my awareness operator has to treat the tree exactly the same way, regardless of whether I am looking at it alone or as part of the forest.

31:56.040 --> 31:58.020
That is precisely what it mandates.

31:58.240 --> 31:58.460
Okay.

31:58.460 --> 32:07.700
And to prove that these four laws are mathematically sound and capable of being fulfilled, MZ introduced a primary example, the Russell operator.

32:08.020 --> 32:11.800
Named after Bertrand Russell, the mathematician who famously broke early set theory.

32:11.940 --> 32:12.580
The very same.

32:12.840 --> 32:18.600
The Russell operator is a function that scans a set and selects only those elements that do not contain themselves.

32:19.080 --> 32:21.560
It specifically filters out the infinite loops.

32:21.740 --> 32:22.200
Oh, interesting.

32:22.200 --> 32:22.780
Mm-hmm.

32:22.920 --> 32:32.440
MZ run the Russell operator through their four laws and mathematically prove that it perfectly satisfies the generation, irreversibility, removal, and selection axioms.

32:32.980 --> 32:42.260
So if we follow their logic, MZ have successfully written a mathematical definition for consciousness and proved that a specific filter algorithm achieves it.

32:42.700 --> 32:44.240
Case closed, we've solved the brain.

32:44.240 --> 32:52.640
Except Flyoxov steps in here with a devastating critique that just pulls the rug out from under the entire mathematical structure.

32:53.280 --> 32:53.880
What did they find?

32:54.100 --> 32:58.420
Flyoxov points out a fatal flaw hidden inside that fourth law, the selection axiom.

32:59.040 --> 33:09.620
By demanding that the awareness of the part must equal the part intersecting with the awareness of the whole, the axiom forces the operator to be utterly blind to context.

33:09.620 --> 33:17.320
Right. If I'm understanding this correctly, the selection axiom forces the math to act like a bouncer at a club checking ID cards.

33:17.700 --> 33:18.020
Exactly.

33:18.440 --> 33:23.680
The bouncer looks at the tree's ID card, and if the card meets the criteria, it gets into the awareness club.

33:24.480 --> 33:29.080
It doesn't matter if the tree is standing alone in a desert or surrounded by a burning forest.

33:29.500 --> 33:34.500
The bouncer makes the decision based purely on the isolated properties of the element itself.

33:34.860 --> 33:37.460
The decision is entirely context independent.

33:37.460 --> 33:40.580
The operator relies on a fixed criterion.

33:41.300 --> 33:49.460
And to highlight how absurd this becomes, Flyxion mathematically proves that the basic well-founded selector W we discussed earlier...

33:49.460 --> 33:52.040
The simple sieve that just deletes looping sets.

33:52.260 --> 33:58.520
Yes, that basic sieve also perfectly satisfies all four of MZ's axioms for consciousness.

33:59.060 --> 34:01.000
Okay, that completely shatters the illusion.

34:01.000 --> 34:12.360
If the W filter, which is literally just a data recovery tool, qualifies as a consciousness operator, then filtering the spam folder out of my email inbox is technically an act of consciousness.

34:12.760 --> 34:13.200
Right.

34:13.380 --> 34:16.940
It doesn't resemble human attention or subjective awareness in any reality.

34:17.380 --> 34:20.840
Because human awareness is defined almost entirely by context.

34:20.840 --> 34:50.820
Exactly.

34:50.840 --> 34:57.380
But under MZ's rigid mathematical axioms, the bouncer at the door doesn't care about the library or the rock concert.

34:57.880 --> 35:00.960
It only checks the isolated properties of the sound wave.

35:00.960 --> 35:03.120
It's totally blind to the room.

35:03.940 --> 35:10.980
This failure highlights why abstract mathematical modeling is deeply dangerous if it is left unchecked by empirical reality.

35:10.980 --> 35:18.960
It is incredibly easy to hide massive, highly questionable subjective assumptions behind a veil of objective-looking equations.

35:19.760 --> 35:23.220
You can dress a philosophical guess up in Greek letters, but it's still a guess.

35:24.220 --> 35:31.100
And Flyxion argues that MZ have fallen headfirst into a well-known philosophical trap called subjectivity as a method.

35:31.560 --> 35:33.060
Subjectivity as a method.

35:33.500 --> 35:35.180
Let's drill into why that is a trap.

35:36.140 --> 35:38.700
It's a defensive maneuver common in the philosophy of mind.

35:39.100 --> 35:51.700
When theorists struggle to explain how the immense objective machinery of the brain produces a localized viewpoint, they often resort to defining experience simply as whatever happens to fall inside their chosen mathematical boundary.

35:51.700 --> 35:53.240
They just draw a circle.

35:53.740 --> 35:57.400
They draw a circle and declare, everything inside this circle is experience.

35:57.800 --> 36:02.420
Everything outside this circle is non-experience, and therefore I do not have to explain it.

36:02.680 --> 36:04.500
It is the ultimate intellectual cop-out.

36:04.900 --> 36:07.300
You rig the definition of the game so that you can't lose.

36:08.220 --> 36:16.600
If someone points out a glaring discrepancy, like the fact that the thema is completely blind to hidden variables causing a massive shift in the network,

36:16.600 --> 36:20.860
you just wave your hand and say, oh, those variables are outside the circle.

36:20.860 --> 36:22.420
Right. They are non-experience.

36:22.580 --> 36:23.360
They aren't my department.

36:23.560 --> 36:23.820
Right.

36:24.220 --> 36:26.580
By doing that, the theory ceases to be science.

36:26.660 --> 36:28.340
It becomes unfalsifiable.

36:29.140 --> 36:35.200
You have protected your mathematical model by shrinking its borders until it has no meaningful contact with the physical universe.

36:35.980 --> 36:40.740
You are no longer making an empirical, testable claim about how the brain functions.

36:41.220 --> 36:45.780
And this is exactly why Flyxion's insistence on dynamical closure is so vital.

36:46.100 --> 36:47.460
It's the antidote to the trap.

36:47.660 --> 36:48.780
Because it tests the boundary.

36:48.780 --> 36:58.460
By testing for dynamical closure, we yank this debate out of the squishy realm of unfalsifiable philosophy and drag it back into the harsh light of testable mechanics.

36:59.040 --> 37:03.420
We don't just politely agree that the thema is a primary experience.

37:03.780 --> 37:06.520
We subject it to an empirical stress test.

37:06.780 --> 37:07.780
We ask the hard question.

37:08.000 --> 37:11.740
Can this localized mathematical shadow actually sustain itself over time?

37:12.140 --> 37:16.260
Does it contain the requisite causal information to dictate its own future state?

37:16.260 --> 37:21.200
Or is it merely a passive screen displaying the output of hidden machinery?

37:21.760 --> 37:26.360
You cannot simply slap a sticker that says consciousness on a set theory filter.

37:26.360 --> 37:32.640
You must demonstrate that the projection behaves as an autonomous, dynamically closed entity.

37:33.300 --> 37:39.780
I want to transition this from the abstract realm of human consciousness down into the concrete reality of what engineers are building today.

37:40.140 --> 37:48.880
Because we are surrounded by artificial systems, large language models, complex AI agents that are designed to mimic a point of view.

37:48.880 --> 37:51.340
They respond with the pronoun I.

37:51.760 --> 37:52.140
They do.

37:52.720 --> 37:58.580
Does this mathematical concept of closure actually have practical applications for evaluating AI?

37:58.980 --> 38:00.240
It absolutely does.

38:00.620 --> 38:06.180
And Flexum devotes a significant portion of the paper, Section 7, to bridging this exact gap.

38:06.720 --> 38:09.560
The section focuses on selection by sufficiency.

38:10.280 --> 38:11.460
Selection by sufficiency.

38:11.460 --> 38:17.220
This is where the abstract set theory meets the muddy trenches of modern machine learning architecture.

38:18.240 --> 38:26.380
To understand the application, Flexum asks us to contrast two fundamentally different mechanisms for how a system selects what to focus on.

38:26.760 --> 38:32.260
Two different ways a network decides what to project into its thema, its compressed viewpoint.

38:32.700 --> 38:35.200
The first approach is selection by ranking.

38:35.820 --> 38:39.540
This is the dominant paradigm in most AI attention mechanisms today.

38:39.540 --> 38:44.960
The network looks at a massive pool of incoming data and ranks it based on salience.

38:45.440 --> 38:46.540
And it looks for what's obvious.

38:47.140 --> 38:53.260
It applies a mathematical weight, and it selects whatever data points are numerically the loudest or most prominent.

38:53.700 --> 38:55.220
The squeaky wheel gets the grease.

38:55.740 --> 39:00.280
But Flychemist points out the fatal logical flaw in this design.

39:00.280 --> 39:11.180
There is absolutely no mathematical guarantee that grabbing the most salient, loudest data point actually provides the information required to predict the future state of the system.

39:11.900 --> 39:13.140
It goes back to the weather analogy.

39:13.640 --> 39:22.780
If the AI is trying to predict the weather, selection by ranking might decide to focus entirely on a massively loud thunderclap because the decibel level is huge.

39:23.180 --> 39:25.260
The math says this is salient.

39:25.260 --> 39:32.520
Meanwhile, it completely ignores a tiny, subtle drop in barometric pressure because the numerical change is small.

39:33.400 --> 39:37.760
But from a causal perspective, the thunderclap is just meaningless noise that has already happened.

39:38.480 --> 39:43.780
The quiet drop in barometric pressure is the critical variable that dictates the storm is coming.

39:44.560 --> 39:47.620
By chasing salience, the projection loses closure.

39:48.060 --> 39:50.100
It becomes blind to its own future.

39:50.540 --> 39:53.280
So if ranking is flawed, what is the alternative?

39:53.620 --> 39:54.560
Yeah, how do they fix it?

39:54.560 --> 39:57.640
The alternative is selection by sufficiency.

39:58.720 --> 40:04.820
A sufficient projection is one that is mathematically proven to be closed under the specific operations of the task.

40:05.360 --> 40:07.560
It doesn't care how loud the data is.

40:07.700 --> 40:09.000
It just looks for what it needs.

40:09.580 --> 40:15.100
It retains exactly the specific variables needed to compute the next step and nothing more.

40:15.320 --> 40:17.580
But crucially, it retains nothing less.

40:17.980 --> 40:19.000
Okay, that makes sense.

40:19.000 --> 40:28.620
To demonstrate the stark difference between these two approaches, Flyxion brings in a highly relevant 2026 machine learning case study conducted by Wang and their colleagues.

40:29.260 --> 40:31.900
The PAR model, progress aligned recurrence.

40:31.900 --> 40:36.300
Let's get into the mechanics of this experiment because it proves the point beautifully.

40:36.940 --> 40:41.860
Wang's team designed an experiment to test sequential state tracking in a looped language model.

40:42.680 --> 40:46.040
You can visualize the task as a highly complex digital shell game.

40:46.380 --> 40:46.940
Shell game.

40:47.060 --> 40:47.260
Okay.

40:47.260 --> 40:55.460
The AI has to track the changing state and location of five distinct boxes over a long series of complicated sequential moves.

40:55.980 --> 41:02.120
So as the game progresses, the AI has to hold the current updated arrangement of the boxes in its active memory.

41:02.520 --> 41:04.700
That arrangement is its projection.

41:05.560 --> 41:08.700
It's the localized viewpoint the AI has of the game state.

41:08.700 --> 41:12.340
The objective of the experiment was to test for a length generalization.

41:12.980 --> 41:17.420
If the engineers trained the AI model to successfully track the boxes for five consecutive moves,

41:17.680 --> 41:22.380
could the model extrapolate those roles and successfully track the boxes for 50 moves?

41:22.520 --> 41:23.760
And what happened when they tested it?

41:24.220 --> 41:31.580
They discovered that if they trained the model using traditional methods where they only provided supervision and error correction at the very end of the five moves,

41:31.780 --> 41:34.400
just telling the AI if the final answer was right or wrong.

41:35.100 --> 41:36.060
Let me guess, it failed.

41:36.460 --> 41:38.140
Extrapolation failed catastrophically.

41:38.140 --> 41:40.900
The model could not track the boxes for 50 moves.

41:41.460 --> 41:44.840
Its internal projection of the game state degraded, lost coherence,

41:44.940 --> 41:48.400
and the AI essentially drifted off into generating random hallucinations.

41:49.300 --> 41:51.660
Because its internal state wasn't dynamically closed.

41:51.980 --> 41:55.300
It was losing tiny, vital pieces of information at every single step,

41:55.400 --> 41:59.800
and without closure, those hidden variables compounded until the system fell apart.

41:59.900 --> 42:00.920
It lacked sufficiency.

42:01.840 --> 42:07.940
Huang's team found that the AI could only achieve true extrapolation under two incredibly strict training conditions.

42:08.140 --> 42:08.760
What were they?

42:08.960 --> 42:11.520
First, the model could not just be checked at the end.

42:11.600 --> 42:16.180
It had to be explicitly, forcibly supervised on its intermediate arrangements.

42:16.820 --> 42:22.120
The engineers had to verify that the projection was perfectly accurate at step one, step two, step three.

42:22.340 --> 42:25.900
The AI had to show its work on the chalkboard at every stage.

42:25.900 --> 42:34.600
And second, the mathematical scale of the recurrent state, the actual physical size of the data package it was permitted to pass to itself in the next loop,

42:34.980 --> 42:36.440
had to be rigidly anchored.

42:36.660 --> 42:37.680
Why does the size matter?

42:38.340 --> 42:40.680
They had to enforce a fixed step map.

42:41.020 --> 42:44.100
The function couldn't just grab whatever data looked salient.

42:44.100 --> 42:48.220
It was mathematically constrained to pass only the sufficient variables.

42:48.940 --> 42:51.920
Let's pull this back to the philosophical claims of Maranker and Zuckerman.

42:52.640 --> 42:56.420
This Wang experiment feels like a direct refutation of their optimism.

42:57.200 --> 43:04.180
MZ sat at a chalkboard, designed their infinite, non-well-founded math, pointed at the thema, crossed their fingers,

43:04.380 --> 43:10.040
and essentially just hoped that their localized viewpoint naturally possessed dynamical closure.

43:10.040 --> 43:13.520
They assume that complexity naturally yields a coherent perspective.

43:14.000 --> 43:17.760
But out in the real world, Wang's engineering team proved the exact opposite.

43:18.460 --> 43:22.900
A coherent, closed viewpoint does not magically emerge just because you have a complex loop.

43:23.480 --> 43:26.340
The AI's projection wanted to drift into chaos.

43:27.080 --> 43:31.060
The engineers had to violently force the model, mathematically constraining it,

43:31.340 --> 43:34.100
to maintain a sufficient, closed projection of the boxes.

43:34.440 --> 43:37.720
That is the immovable reality of system dynamics.

43:37.720 --> 43:43.900
If a system is going to track a complex sequence, whether it's an AI tracking five digital boxes

43:43.900 --> 43:46.120
or a human brain tracking a conversation,

43:46.520 --> 43:53.940
its internal viewpoint must be actively, forcefully shaped by the architecture to retain task-sufficient distinctions.

43:54.240 --> 43:55.640
You can't just build it in hope.

43:56.120 --> 43:56.380
No.

43:56.700 --> 43:59.900
You cannot just construct a massive hall of mathematical mirrors,

44:00.340 --> 44:03.200
point to the infinite reflections, and declare you have built a mind.

44:03.200 --> 44:08.420
It has to be structured to hold the specific data it needs to survive the next millisecond.

44:09.080 --> 44:12.240
Which brings us perfectly to the final phase of our deep dive.

44:12.760 --> 44:17.060
Because as compelling as Flyxion's framework is for analyzing these systems,

44:17.760 --> 44:22.020
Flyxion is also remarkably intellectually honest about the boundaries of this math.

44:22.880 --> 44:26.420
Before we get swept away and assume we have just mapped the soul,

44:26.800 --> 44:30.180
we have to look at the hard limitations detailed in Section 8 of the paper.

44:30.180 --> 44:36.420
It is critical to establish what mathematics can and cannot do in the realm of cognitive science.

44:37.100 --> 44:42.920
Flyxion outlines three major foundational limits to the MZ framework and to set theory models in general.

44:43.500 --> 44:44.140
What's the first one?

44:44.660 --> 44:46.880
The first limitation is one of causality.

44:47.420 --> 44:49.060
No new causal mechanism.

44:49.540 --> 44:50.440
Let's expand on that.

44:50.620 --> 44:55.920
Because we spent a lot of time discussing how the abstract set theory math updates in perfect harmony

44:55.920 --> 44:58.660
with the physical voltages of the McCullough-Pitts neurons.

44:58.820 --> 44:59.680
They are synchronized.

44:59.680 --> 45:04.500
But Flyxion emphasizes that a consistent mathematical re-description of a physical event

45:04.500 --> 45:08.040
does not conjure a new physical cause into existence.

45:08.560 --> 45:13.920
The fact that we can translate the firing of neurons into a breathtakingly elegant equation of circular sets

45:13.920 --> 45:17.520
does not mean the circular sets are now driving the brain.

45:17.760 --> 45:25.340
So if I hire a surveyor to draw an incredibly precise, mathematically perfect map of downtown traffic patterns,

45:25.340 --> 45:28.280
complete with flow rates and bottleneck algorithms,

45:28.280 --> 45:32.800
the map is highly accurate, but the map is not causing the traffic.

45:33.280 --> 45:37.100
The cars are still driven by combustion engines and frustrated commuters.

45:37.620 --> 45:39.660
The territory remains unchanged by the map.

45:40.320 --> 45:43.820
The physical neurons are still firing based on their threshold voltages,

45:43.820 --> 45:48.900
entirely ignorant of the fact that we have assigned them non-well-founded set decorations.

45:49.640 --> 45:54.960
Changing the descriptive language from electrical spikes to thematic awareness operators

45:54.960 --> 45:59.640
injects absolutely zero new physical agency into the biological system.

45:59.640 --> 46:05.200
Which flows seamlessly into the second major limitation, no ontological division.

46:05.600 --> 46:10.320
And when I read this section, it felt like Flyxion was taking direct aim at the ghost of Rene Descartes

46:10.320 --> 46:13.100
and the entire history of philosophical dualism.

46:13.100 --> 46:15.600
It is an outright rejection of dualism.

46:16.380 --> 46:21.740
In their foundational papers, Maranker and Suckerman made a very specific, highly controversial framing choice.

46:22.100 --> 46:25.900
They posited that their mathematical sets, the memes and themas,

46:26.380 --> 46:29.540
resided in what they called a platonic virtual space.

46:29.860 --> 46:34.660
They treated the physical wetware of the brain and the virtual mathematical space of the mind

46:34.660 --> 46:39.000
as two distinct, ontologically separate realms of existence.

46:39.400 --> 46:41.200
The classic ghost in the machine.

46:41.200 --> 46:47.720
The physical brain is the hardware and the mathematical mind is this ethereal software floating in a different dimension.

46:48.520 --> 46:51.020
But Flyxion systematically dismantles this.

46:51.620 --> 46:55.560
Flyxion argues that pointing out a difference between two mathematical representations,

46:55.860 --> 47:00.480
say, the difference between a finite voltage array and an infinite circular set,

47:00.940 --> 47:04.620
does not grant you the right to split the actual universe in two.

47:05.380 --> 47:09.680
A shift in mathematical perspective does not create a new ontological category.

47:09.680 --> 47:13.460
So, just because Axel's math looks weird and infinite,

47:13.940 --> 47:16.560
it doesn't mean it's tapping into a magical soul dimension.

47:17.060 --> 47:21.980
It's just a different way of writing down the exact same physical feedback loop happening in the physical skull.

47:22.240 --> 47:22.820
Precisely.

47:23.180 --> 47:25.620
Emes leads to the third and final limitation,

47:26.020 --> 47:31.420
which addresses a very specific technical misunderstanding that MZ used to justify that dualism.

47:31.420 --> 47:34.360
The limitation of computability.

47:34.680 --> 47:35.240
Computability.

47:35.740 --> 47:38.840
Marenker and Zuckerman looked at Axel's anti-foundation sets,

47:39.080 --> 47:40.560
saw that they looped infinitely,

47:40.840 --> 47:43.520
and concluded that they lacked effective construction.

47:43.900 --> 47:46.360
Meaning you couldn't build them step by step from the bottom up,

47:46.420 --> 47:47.820
because there is no bottom.

47:48.000 --> 47:48.220
Right.

47:48.760 --> 47:51.100
And because they believe the sets couldn't be constructed,

47:51.520 --> 47:55.440
they assumed these infinite structures were beyond the reach of physical computation.

47:55.440 --> 47:59.900
They thought they had found mathematical proof that the mind was performing operations

47:59.900 --> 48:02.860
that a physical computer fundamentally could not do.

48:03.320 --> 48:08.060
It fit perfectly into the mystical, non-physical aura of the platonic realm.

48:08.540 --> 48:11.660
But Flyxion corrects the record here using computational theory.

48:12.000 --> 48:12.320
How so?

48:12.620 --> 48:17.280
Flyxion points to a landmark 1987 computer science paper by Page and Tarjan.

48:17.700 --> 48:22.700
The paper proves that just because a mathematical set loops infinitely in abstract theory

48:22.700 --> 48:24.920
does not mean a computer cannot manage it.

48:25.500 --> 48:28.900
If the infinite loop is generated by a finite network of nodes,

48:29.140 --> 48:31.340
like a brain with a finite number of neurons,

48:31.520 --> 48:34.360
it is entirely computationally tractable.

48:34.800 --> 48:38.300
So you don't need to unfold the infinite loop to understand its structure.

48:38.560 --> 48:40.120
To use the mirror analogy again,

48:40.220 --> 48:43.160
if I want to understand the optics of the infinite reflections,

48:43.380 --> 48:45.960
I don't need to physically walk down the infinite hallway.

48:45.960 --> 48:50.140
I just need a tape measure to calculate the exact angle and distance

48:50.140 --> 48:52.540
between the two physical mirrors standing in front of me.

48:52.700 --> 48:57.660
The infinite complexity is generated by a finite, measurable, physical blueprint.

48:58.280 --> 49:01.480
And Page and Tarjan provided the algorithm to measure that blueprint.

49:01.940 --> 49:03.840
It is called partition refinement.

49:04.420 --> 49:08.500
It's an algorithmic method for determining structural bisimilarity.

49:08.720 --> 49:11.460
You start by assuming all nodes might be identical,

49:11.860 --> 49:13.060
placing them in one giant room.

49:13.420 --> 49:14.740
Then you look at their labels,

49:15.340 --> 49:17.120
and you partition them into smaller rooms.

49:17.680 --> 49:19.440
Then you look at where they point,

49:19.880 --> 49:21.200
and you partition them again.

49:21.200 --> 49:24.840
You keep refining the groups until no more splits can be made.

49:25.400 --> 49:28.840
It's an entirely finite, step-by-step computational process.

49:29.060 --> 49:31.040
It is perfectly decidable.

49:31.580 --> 49:34.820
Therefore, using anti-foundation mathematics does not prove

49:34.820 --> 49:37.800
that the brain is engaging in magical hypercomputation.

49:38.280 --> 49:42.800
It is executing complex, but entirely standard, finite graph processing.

49:42.800 --> 49:46.000
The mystery is stripped away, leaving only the mechanics.

49:46.440 --> 49:48.780
So, if we pull all of these threads together,

49:49.580 --> 49:52.200
what is the final verdict on Flyxion's analysis?

49:53.040 --> 49:55.780
We have this incredibly robust mathematical tool,

49:56.160 --> 49:57.680
the test for dynamical closure.

49:58.220 --> 49:59.580
But as we've established,

50:00.260 --> 50:05.080
the math doesn't automatically prove that a conscious mind exists inside the network.

50:05.080 --> 50:09.180
The ultimate verdict is that the model must be empirically tested.

50:10.060 --> 50:13.780
The question is no longer whether MZ's equations look elegant on a chalkboard.

50:14.280 --> 50:16.620
The open, unanswered scientific question is,

50:17.040 --> 50:19.840
which specific physical network architectures,

50:19.940 --> 50:22.960
and which localized nodes within those networks,

50:23.440 --> 50:28.520
actually managed to satisfy the mathematical condition of dynamical closure in reality?

50:29.280 --> 50:32.820
Flyxion has handed the scientific community a rigorous diagnostic tool.

50:33.580 --> 50:37.900
Now, the burden of proof shifts to the neuroscientists mapping the connectome

50:37.900 --> 50:40.720
and the AI researchers building language models.

50:41.020 --> 50:42.980
They have to go out into the field

50:42.980 --> 50:46.160
and find a system that actually passes the closure test.

50:46.500 --> 50:49.540
They have to find the shadow that can predict its own future.

50:49.940 --> 50:52.200
Which brings us to the end of our journey today.

50:52.760 --> 50:56.780
Let's synthesize this massive dive into abstract mathematics

50:56.780 --> 51:00.100
and bring it back down to what it actually means for you,

51:00.200 --> 51:01.660
the person listening to this right now.

51:02.520 --> 51:07.460
We started by looking at how the infinite looping feedback circuits of a neural network

51:07.460 --> 51:12.500
force us to abandon traditional math and embrace Axel's circular sets.

51:13.200 --> 51:17.360
We examined the fragile magic trick of using a mathematical sieve

51:17.360 --> 51:20.120
to recover physical data from an infinite loop.

51:20.780 --> 51:23.980
We tracked how those static equations are forced to evolve over time,

51:23.980 --> 51:28.360
highlighting the severe, often unrecognized loss of information

51:28.360 --> 51:31.680
that occurs when a massive system is compressed into a single viewpoint.

51:32.440 --> 51:36.320
And we confronted the philosophical danger of mistaking that compressed,

51:36.560 --> 51:39.400
leaky shadow for a robust, autonomous consciousness.

51:39.400 --> 51:43.560
The ultimate takeaway from Flyxion's work is a profound re-evaluation

51:43.560 --> 51:46.060
of what a point of view actually is.

51:47.000 --> 51:50.080
A subjective experience is only scientifically meaningful

51:50.080 --> 51:54.260
and computationally functional if it possesses dynamical closure,

51:54.720 --> 51:59.040
if it contains the requisite information to accurately chart its own trajectory.

51:59.540 --> 52:02.640
If it lacks that closure, it is not a complete mind.

52:02.940 --> 52:05.280
It is simply an incomplete lossy snapshot,

52:05.280 --> 52:09.660
a projection entirely at the mercy of a deeper, unseen physical reality.

52:09.660 --> 52:13.680
And I want to leave you with a final, highly personal way to apply this concept.

52:14.720 --> 52:17.120
Think about your own internal monologue right now.

52:17.940 --> 52:19.500
The voice in your head listening to this.

52:20.420 --> 52:23.640
We all possess a deep-seated intuition that our conscious thoughts

52:23.640 --> 52:25.340
are the ultimate authors of our destiny.

52:25.940 --> 52:30.540
That our localized thema is in absolute control of our future actions.

52:31.220 --> 52:33.800
But if our conscious mind is in reality

52:33.800 --> 52:37.400
just a highly compressed, incredibly lossy projection

52:37.400 --> 52:41.800
of the billions of parallel processes happening in our entire physical brain,

52:42.440 --> 52:45.680
a projection that might fundamentally lack dynamical closure,

52:46.280 --> 52:48.960
maybe that explains the friction of being human.

52:49.820 --> 52:52.320
It reframes the concept of self-control entirely.

52:52.740 --> 52:54.960
Think about the times you constantly surprise yourself.

52:55.280 --> 52:56.220
When you break a diet,

52:56.580 --> 52:58.940
even when you swore an oath to yourself that you wouldn't.

52:59.440 --> 53:01.920
Or when you suddenly snap in anger at a friend

53:01.920 --> 53:05.480
despite your absolute best conscious intentions to remain calm.

53:05.480 --> 53:08.420
We tend to view those moments as moral failings.

53:09.240 --> 53:10.540
But mathematically speaking,

53:11.220 --> 53:13.380
maybe they are just failures of closure.

53:13.980 --> 53:16.380
The massive hidden parts of your neural network,

53:16.920 --> 53:19.800
the raw, uncompressed barometric pressure data

53:19.800 --> 53:22.260
that never made it into your conscious weather forecast,

53:22.620 --> 53:23.400
simply took over.

53:24.100 --> 53:26.800
Your localized thema didn't have the full picture.

53:27.360 --> 53:29.400
It couldn't accurately predict its own future

53:29.400 --> 53:32.080
because it was acting on incomplete information.

53:32.080 --> 53:35.340
You were being driven by variables you didn't even know existed.

53:36.100 --> 53:37.840
So, until our next deep dive,

53:38.400 --> 53:40.420
try to keep an eye on your hidden variables.

